<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matsudaira Yoshinaga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a>–<a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Matsudaira Shungaku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matsudaira Yoshinaga, known as Shungaku, was lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> domain and a leading reformer of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. He employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hashimoto-sanai">Hashimoto Sanai</a> and Yokoi Shonan and promoted Western learning, medicine and trade. In 1862 he became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s director of political affairs, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> he served briefly in the new government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matsudaira-yoshinaga.jpg" alt="Matsudaira Yoshinaga" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 不明。 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matudaira_Syungaku.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yoshinaga was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a>, the eighth son of Tokugawa Narimasa, head of the Tayasu-Tokugawa, one of the gosankyō cadet branches of the Tokugawa clan. Intended for adoption into the Iyo-Matsuyama Domain, he was instead chosen as daimyō of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> when its young lord, Matsudaira Narisawa, died without an heir on 27 July 1838. He took a character of his name from Shōgun Tokugawa Ieyoshi, and in 1839 married Yu-hime, a daughter of Hosokawa Narimori of Kumamoto Domain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> he cut the stipends of his retainers by half for three years and his own expenses for five, set up a translation bureau for Western learning, an armaments factory and the Meidōkan school, and joined the domain with wealthy merchants in a trading venture. After the Perry Expedition of 1853 he first sided with the anti-foreign party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-nariaki">Tokugawa Nariaki</a>, then came round to opening the country. He backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a> in the shogunal succession, protested at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naosuke">Ii Naosuke</a>'s signing of the Treaty of Amity and Commerce without the emperor's assent, and on 5 July 1858 was forced to resign in the Ansei purge, taking the name Shungaku.</p><p>After Ii's assassination he returned in April 1862 and, as Seiji sōsaishoku, supported the kōbu gattai union of court and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>. He formed the Rōshigumi, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinsengumi">Shinsengumi</a>, and invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yokoi-shonan">Yokoi Shōnan</a> as an adviser, in what was called the Bunkyū Renovation. Pressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonno-joi">Sonnō jōi</a> movement, he resigned and in 1863 planned a march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> that never took place. In 1864 he sat in the short-lived Sanyo Kaigi council of great lords, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> he acted as an intermediary until 1869.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the Meiji government he held several high posts, including Chief Executive of Internal Affairs, but resigned in protest at the dominance of men from Chōshū and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>. In 1870 he invited William Elliot Griffis to teach in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a>, and in 1881 he helped compile the Tokugawa reiten roku, a record of shogunal ritual. He is counted among the Four Wise Lords of the Bakumatsu period and is credited with naming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji era</a>. He died in 1890 and is buried at Kaian-ji in Shinagawa, Tokyo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matsudaira Yoshinaga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 19th century <b>Matsudaira Yoshinaga</b>, known as Shungaku, employed Hashimoto Sanai and Yokoi Shonan, promoted Western learning, medicine and trade, and in 1862 became the shogunate's director of political affairs, urging reform.</blockquote><a href="/state/fukui-domain">Fukui Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Koishikawa-ku, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.69,139.75&amp;year=1868" aria-label="Edo Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8079 874 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Matsudaira Yoshinaga"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8529 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8529 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.69,139.75&amp;year=1868">What was here in 1868? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
